<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>Two boda boda riders are stopped at a checkpoint. The probability that the first rider has a valid licence is <m>{0.6}</m>, and for the second rider it is <m>{0.55}</m>. These events are independent.</p><p>Give each probability exactly, as a fraction or an exact decimal.</p><p>(a) Find the probability that both riders have a valid licence.</p><div><fillin characters="8" name="ans1"/>  </div><p>(b) Find the probability that neither rider has a valid licence.</p><div><fillin characters="8" name="ans2"/>  </div><p>(c) Find the probability that at least one rider has a valid licence.</p><div><fillin characters="8" name="ans3"/>  </div><p>(d) Find the probability that exactly one rider has a valid licence.</p><div><fillin characters="8" name="ans4"/>  </div></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>(a) Both: <m>{0.6} \times {0.55} = {0.33}</m>.</p><p>(b) Neither: <m>(1 - {0.6}) \times (1 - {0.55}) = {0.4} \times {0.45} = {0.18}</m>.</p><p>(c) <q>At least one</q> is everything except <q>neither</q>: <m>1 - {0.18} = {0.82}</m>.</p><p>(d) Exactly one is the first only or the second only: <m>{0.6} \times {0.45} + {0.4} \times {0.55} = {0.27} + {0.22} = {0.49}</m>. Check: both, exactly one and neither add to <m>{0.33} + {0.49} + {0.18} = 1</m>.</p></p></solution>
    <answer><p><m>\frac{33}{100}</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>\frac{9}{50}</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>\frac{41}{50}</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>\frac{49}{100}</m></p></answer>
</stack-static>